module «Ackermann function»

// Rosetta Code — Ackermann function, written on the ENGLISH keyword surface.
// The same program as `ackermann-function.flang`, keyword for keyword.
//
// A(0, n) = n + 1; A(m, 0) = A(m−1, 1); A(m, n) = A(m−1, A(m, n−1)).
//
// This task is in the set not for the function but for what it shows about the
// boundary of the language — and it shows it better than any explanation.
//
// The Ackermann function TERMINATES on every pair of non-negative integers.
// That is proved mathematically: the pair (m, n) decreases lexicographically,
// and the lexicographic order on pairs of naturals is well-founded. The proof
// is not hard, but it reasons about natural numbers.
//
// flang does not carry out that reasoning. Its termination analysis knows
// exactly one argument: the argument of the recursive call is a structural PART
// of the caller's argument (the tail of a list, a field of a record, a field of
// a variant). Numbers have no parts. So this file honestly says `function` and
// not `total`, and writing `total` yields FLANG_NOT_TOTAL.
//
// Hence the point worth taking away: `total` does NOT mean «terminates». It
// means «termination is proved BY THIS analysis». The converse does not hold,
// and Ackermann is the best-known witness. A language pretending that its class
// of total functions coincides with the class of terminating ones is lying: by
// Rice's theorem no such analysis exists at all, and every real one draws the
// line somewhere earlier. The only question is whether the line is named.
//
// Fuel does not help here. In merge sort and in the sieve the depth of the
// recursion is at most the length of the input list, and the list serves as
// fuel. In Ackermann the depth grows faster than any primitive recursive
// function: A(4, 2) is a power of two with 19 729 digits. A list of that length
// does not exist, and not because of memory: there is no way to build it.

// Ordinary — for the reason in the module header. Writing `total` is not
// allowed.
function «Ackermann»
  accepts m: number, n: number
  returns number
  ensures «at m zero Ackermann just adds one» if m equals 0 then (result equals (n plus 1)) else true
  ensures «at n zero the step goes with one» if not (m equals 0) then (if n equals 0 then (result equals («Ackermann» of (m minus 1) and 1)) else true) else true
  example «A(0, 0) = 1»
    given m equals 0
    given n equals 0
    expected 1
  example «A(0, 4) = 5 — the bottom row simply adds one»
    given m equals 0
    given n equals 4
    expected 5
  example «A(1, 1) = 3»
    given m equals 1
    given n equals 1
    expected 3
  example «A(1, 5) = 7 — the first row is n + 2»
    given m equals 1
    given n equals 5
    expected 7
  example «A(2, 2) = 7»
    given m equals 2
    given n equals 2
    expected 7
  example «A(2, 4) = 11 — the second row is 2n + 3»
    given m equals 2
    given n equals 4
    expected 11
  example «A(3, 3) = 61 — the third row is a power of two less three»
    given m equals 3
    given n equals 3
    expected 61
  example «A(3, 5) = 253»
    given m equals 3
    given n equals 5
    expected 253
  if m equals 0
    then n plus 1
    else
      if n equals 0
        then «Ackermann» of (m minus 1) and 1
        else «Ackermann» of (m minus 1) and («Ackermann» of m and (n minus 1))

// A closed form for the first three rows — it IS total, because there is no
// recursion in it at all. Standing next to the recursive version it shows that
// the matter is not the values: the very same number is computed provably by
// one function and not by the other. The only difference is how the
// computation is written.
total function «Ackermann in closed form»
  accepts m: number, n: number
  returns number
  example «Row zero»
    given m equals 0
    given n equals 4
    expected 5
  example «Row one»
    given m equals 1
    given n equals 5
    expected 7
  example «Row two»
    given m equals 2
    given n equals 4
    expected 11
  example «Beyond the first three rows — zero, there is nothing to compute with»
    given m equals 3
    given n equals 3
    expected 0
  if m equals 0
    then n plus 1
    else
      if m equals 1
        then n plus 2
        else
          if m equals 2 then (2 times n) plus 3 else 0

// A claim: on the first three rows the recursive and the closed form agree.
// Ordinary — it calls an ordinary function.
function «The two forms agree»
  accepts m: number, n: number
  returns boolean
  example «Row zero»
    given m equals 0
    given n equals 4
    expected true
  example «Row one»
    given m equals 1
    given n equals 5
    expected true
  example «Row two»
    given m equals 2
    given n equals 6
    expected true
  («Ackermann» of m and n) equals («Ackermann in closed form» of m and n)
